WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.610
Everyone loves a surprise cameo; they make
every movie that much more fun. But there

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:09.080
are some cameos so well hidden—because they're
a special favor to a star or director, or

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.440
some kind of elaborate inside joke—that
they're easy to miss. Here are some times

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.990
that big stars showed up in secret uncredited
roles…and hardly anyone noticed.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.970
Glenn Close, Hook

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:26.020
During one unforgettable scene in Steven Spielberg's
Peter Pan riff, Captain Hook brutally throws

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:30.480
one of his own pirates, Gutless, into "the
boo box" with a scorpion. It's enough to make

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.840
any self-respecting pirate scream like a girl
— but in this pirate's case, he actually

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.329
was one. That's actress Glenn Close under
the bushy beard, decades before she earned

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:43.739
an Oscar nomination for playing another male
character in Albert Nobbs.

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:45.230
Peter Jackson, Hot Fuzz

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:49.809
At the beginning of this movie, Simon Pegg's
cop character gets stabbed in the hand by

00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:55.249
an unhinged Santa Claus. Santa's face is obscured
by a beard, but that psychotic Father Christmas

00:00:55.249 --> 00:00:59.600
is Lord of the Rings director Peter Jackson,
who was friendly with Hot Fuzz director Edgar

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.140
Wright. After Jackson invited Wright to visit
the set of King Kong, Wright returned the

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.370
favor by extending an invite to the set of
his film, where Jackson volunteered to do

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:12.750
a cameo. The scene was shot after Jackson
had lost a great deal of weight and shaved

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:17.480
his beard, which means the director wore a
phony fat suit for his big moment onscreen.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.140
Cate Blanchett, Hot Fuzz

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:24.730
Peter Jackson wasn't the only big Hollywood
name to pop up in this British cop comedy.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:29.690
In another scene, Simon Pegg's character meets
up with his ex-girlfriend, an investigator

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.200
named Janine—who's played by Cate Blanchett.
Blanchett was a big fan of Edgar Wright's

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.820
zombie comedy Shaun of the Dead, and when
she met him at a party, he offered her a cameo…

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.350
only one in which her face would be totally
obscured, just because he thought it would

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:46.370
be funny to cast an Oscar winner in a role
where she'd be completely unrecognizable.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.390
Danny Glover, Maverick

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:53.620
In this 1994 big-screen remake of a classic
TV comic western, Maverick is in a bank when

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.229
a robbery goes down. Leading the crooks is
a guy who uses a red bandana to cover up his

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:02.430
face—he wouldn't want to be noticed, after
all. The scarf also did a pretty good job

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:07.409
of concealing the identity of the actor playing
the bandit: Mel Gibson's Lethal Weapon co-star

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:08.500
Danny Glover.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.739
Stephen Spielberg, The Blues Brothers

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:17.480
This film featured a marvelous roster of legendary
blues and R&amp;B musicians including Ray Charles,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:23.290
Aretha Franklin, James Brown, Cab Calloway,
and John Lee Hooker. But also making an appearance

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:28.049
is director Steven Spielberg, who struck up
a friendship with Dan Akroyd and John Belushi

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:32.560
a year earlier and appears here as a clerk
in a county assessor's office.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.829
Dan Akroyd, Indiana Jones and the Temple of
Doom

00:02:35.829 --> 00:02:40.829
After Steven Spielberg cameoed in Dan Aykroyd's
movie, Aykroyd returned the favor. At the

00:02:40.829 --> 00:02:45.180
very beginning of the second Indiana Jones
adventure, a mustachioed British man helps

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.519
arrange transportation for Indy at an airport
— with one minor caveat.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.440
"There might be a slight inconvenience as
you will be riding on a cargo full of live

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:55.440
poultry."

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:00.499
Of course, not even a silly accent can entirely
mask Akroyd's distinctive voice, which makes

00:03:00.499 --> 00:03:03.760
this one cameo that's easier to spot than
the rest.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.560
Bill Murray, Dumb and Dumber To

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.170
This sequel to the classic idiot flick Dumb
and Dumber was written and directed by Bobby

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:15.569
and Peter Farrelly, who also directed Bill
Murray in their 1996 bowling comedy Kingpin.

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:19.889
Like Hot Fuzz director Edgar Wright, the Farrelly
brothers thought it would be amusing to cast

00:03:19.889 --> 00:03:25.439
a very well-known actor in the role of Icepick,
Harry's roommate, whose identity is completely

00:03:25.439 --> 00:03:29.819
concealed with a breathing apparatus throughout
his one scene — and Murray was happy to

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:33.359
trade his Kingpin combover for a hazmat suit.

00:03:33.359 --> 00:03:35.609
Jimmy Buffett, Jurassic World

00:03:35.609 --> 00:03:39.780
When order inevitably breaks down and the
dinosaurs at Jurassic World escape to terrorize

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.359
the theme park guests, there's a few seconds
of comic relief: an old dude in an orange

00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:49.140
shirt runs away from the chaos and carnage…while
carefully carrying away two margaritas. Who

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.970
played this hero? The planet's most ardent
margarita lover, of course: "Margaritaville"

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:55.739
singer Jimmy Buffett.

00:03:55.739 --> 00:03:58.379
Stephen Colbert, The Hobbit: The Desolation
of Smaug

00:03:58.379 --> 00:04:03.999
Stephen Colbert is a huge J.R.R. Tolkien fan
and scholar, having shown off his geek credentials

00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:06.989
multiple times on The Colbert Report and The
Late Show.

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:11.269
"Last time you were here, we had a Tolkien
showdown and I, I smoked you like a ham."

00:04:11.269 --> 00:04:16.430
In 2013, he was rewarded for his devotion
with a small part in the second installment

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:21.390
of Peter Jackson's The Hobbit trilogy. Colbert
appears onscreen for just a few seconds as

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.260
one of Lake-town's spies.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:25.190
Owen Wilson, Rushmore

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:30.060
Wilson co-wrote this indie dramedy cult classic
with childhood friend and frequent collaborator

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:35.030
Wes Anderson, and his two actor brothers show
up in minor roles. Andrew plays a coach at

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:39.610
the titular school, and Luke plays a doctor
who goes on a date with Rosemary, played by

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.310
Olivia Williams, with whom most of the men
in the movie are totally obsessed.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:44.740
"These are OR scrubs."

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.040
"O R they?"

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.850
But Owen is in the film, too, if you look
closely: he plays Edward Appleby, Rosemary's

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:57.340
deceased husband, who appears exclusively
in a couple pictures on the wall in Rosemary's

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:58.340
house.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.230
Basically everyone in Star Wars: The Force
Awakens

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:06.160
When the new installment of this beloved franchise
hit theaters in 2015, it was full of secret

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.660
cameos from actors who just wanted to be a
part of the fun. So when Rey trades salvaged

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:15.230
parts to this Jakku junk dealer in exchange
for rations, there's actually a familiar face

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:21.060
under all that latex, padding, and CGI: actor,
writer, and total Star Wars superfan Simon

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.060
Pegg.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:26.040
And he's not the only one! Remember the stormtrooper
who releases Rey in this pivotal scene?

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.430
"And you'll drop your weapon!"

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:30.820
"And I'll drop my weapon."

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:35.520
That's James Bond himself, Daniel Craig, trading
his dinner jacket for a suit of white plastic

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.780
armor. And a final surprise: throughout the
movie, you're hearing but not seeing a famous

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.690
comedian. The little chirps and blips made
by BB-8, the world's most adorable droid,

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:49.310
are actually the vocalizations of actor and
Saturday Night Live alumnus Bill Hader.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:53.970
Thanks for watching! Click the Looper icon
to subscribe to our YouTube channel. Plus

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.910
check out all this cool stuff we know you'll
love, too!

